 The artifact selected for this enhancement is a full-stack development module created six months ago. Full-stack development involves building both the frontend (client-side/user interface) and the backend (server-side/database) of applications. Full-stack development ensures smooth data communication between the front and back ends through APIs, managing databases, and debugging across the entire stack.  The front end that the user sees and interacts with, including HTML and CSS. The backend includes the server, application logic, and databases such as Node.js, MySQL, and MongoDB. The most common and professional stack to use for this description is JavaScript for the front end and Node.js for the back end. 
The source code used synchronous form submissions which often froze the user interface while waiting for server responses. By refactoring a monolithic, synchronous process into a modular, asynchronous workflow, I demonstrate how modern applications are useful to maintain performance and responsiveness.  I chose to include this specific topic in my ePortfolio because it serves as a connection for basic functional programming and professional-grade software engineering.
The enhancement process focused on three specific components showcase my understanding in software engineering. First, I implemented modularity by decoupling the API logic from the user interface components. This ensures the code is easier to test and maintain reflecting industry-standard design patterns. Second, I integrated asynchronous http post requests using the async/await pattern. This shift is critical for user experience, as it keeps the application interactive while data is processed in the background. Finally, I incorporated robust state management and error handling. Furthermore, all enhanced codes are well-organized, with proper comments and indentation. 
As shown in my design flowchart, the application now dynamically updates its internal state based on the server’s response, either redirecting the user to the admin dashboard or displaying a specific error message if the "No" path is taken. 
 
2. The Software Design and Engineering   enhancement diagram
For Milestone Two, this enhancement directly addresses Program Outcome One: Design, develop, and implement computing solutions that demonstrate software engineering practices. Throughout working on this enhancement I  met the course outcomes planned specifically those related to designing and evaluating computing solutions. This enhancement allowed me to meet the course outcomes for Software Design and Engineering by demonstrating my ability to implement industry-standard design patterns. By refactoring a monolithic, synchronous process into a modular, asynchronous workflow. I am able to demonstrate how modern applications maintain performance and responsiveness under real-world conditions. 
The process of enhancing this artifact, the most significant learning occurred while refactoring the original synchronous logic into a modular, asynchronous architecture. In my initial version of this project, a single function handled the UI, data validation, and the network request. By breaking these responsibilities apart into a dedicated apiService, I learned how decoupling code makes a system significantly easier to debug and scale. 
The primary challenge I faced was ensuring the application state remained consistent throughout the execution of the asynchronous request. To solve this I implemented defensive programming within try/catch blocks to prevent the app from crashing during network failures.
